\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.0.1}{\ignorespaces Chapter~28 on the six-step ladder: hand-running the AHU fold and spotting the wrong-root trap is \(\textcolor {lv3}{\textbf  {L3}}\); the coded centre-rooted pairing (1800G) and interned-id counting are \(\textcolor {lv4}{\textbf  {L4}}\); the hash-set merge (601D) is \(\textcolor {lv5}{\textbf  {L5}}\); the Summit (CF 1252F, every component the same shape) is step~6.}}{1656}{figure.caption.278}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.0.2}{\ignorespaces The running example \(R\) (9 nodes, root 1). The two orange subtrees are isomorphic to each other (each is ``a node above two leaves''); subtree\((4)\) (a node above one leaf) is isomorphic to neither. Mirror pairing at the root and the AHU ids of all nine subtrees are traced across 28.1 and 28.3.}}{1657}{figure.caption.279}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c22-tree}{{28.0.2}{1657}{The running example \(R\) (9 nodes, root 1). The two orange subtrees are isomorphic to each other (each is ``a node above two leaves''); subtree\((4)\) (a node above one leaf) is isomorphic to neither. Mirror pairing at the root and the AHU ids of all nine subtrees are traced across 28.1 and 28.3}{figure.caption.279}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.0.3}{\ignorespaces The look-alike pair \((T_1,T_2\)): same invariants, different trees. Every cheap invariant says yes: same size, same degree sequence \((3,2,2,2,1,1,1)\), same diameter \(5\), same centre edge \(\{3,4\}\) (red labels mark the unique degree-\(3\) vertex). The decisive read is the distance profile from that vertex to the leaves, \(\{1,2,3\}\) against \(\{1,1,4\}\) -- isomorphisms preserve distances, so no bijection exists. \(\S  \ref {sec:c22-centre}\) computes the same verdict mechanically from centre-rooted canonical names, and that machinery also catches the opposite error (\(R\) versus \(R'\): isomorphic, yet wrong-rooted names disagree).}}{1657}{figure.caption.280}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c22-trap}{{28.0.3}{1657}{The look-alike pair \((T_1,T_2\)): same invariants, different trees. Every cheap invariant says yes: same size, same degree sequence \((3,2,2,2,1,1,1)\), same diameter \(5\), same centre edge \(\{3,4\}\) (red labels mark the unique degree-\(3\) vertex). The decisive read is the distance profile from that vertex to the leaves, \(\{1,2,3\}\) against \(\{1,1,4\}\) -- isomorphisms preserve distances, so no bijection exists. \(\secref {c22-centre}\) computes the same verdict mechanically from centre-rooted canonical names, and that machinery also catches the opposite error (\(R\) versus \(R'\): isomorphic, yet wrong-rooted names disagree)}{figure.caption.280}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.0.4}{\ignorespaces The chapter's five-state taxonomy. \emph  {intern} is the deterministic AHU fold; \emph  {center} lifts it to unrooted trees via the canonical root; \emph  {hash} trades determinism for \(O(1)\) compares; \emph  {count} uses ids as shape names; \emph  {reject} hands the job to the right neighbour. Each solved problem sits on one row: CF 1800G on \emph  {center}+pairing, CF 601D on \emph  {hash}+count, the Summit CF 1252F on \emph  {hash}+center at the edge.}}{1658}{figure.caption.281}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c22-taxonomy}{{28.0.4}{1658}{The chapter's five-state taxonomy. \emph {intern} is the deterministic AHU fold; \emph {center} lifts it to unrooted trees via the canonical root; \emph {hash} trades determinism for \(O(1)\) compares; \emph {count} uses ids as shape names; \emph {reject} hands the job to the right neighbour. Each solved problem sits on one row: CF 1800G on \emph {center}+pairing, CF 601D on \emph {hash}+count, the Summit CF 1252F on \emph {hash}+center at the edge}{figure.caption.281}{}}
