\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {28.3.3}Two centres: why ``pick one'' is not a rule}{1674}{subsection.28.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.3.1}{\ignorespaces The centre-edge trap, with a real counterexample. \(Q_1\) has edges \(1\!-\!2,2\!-\!3,3\!-\!4,4\!-\!5,4\!-\!6,6\!-\!7\); \(Q_2\) has \(1\!-\!2,2\!-\!4,4\!-\!3,3\!-\!5,3\!-\!6,6\!-\!7\). Both centres are the edge \(\{3,4\}\) (red), and \(Q_1\cong  Q_2\): the bijection that swaps \(3\) and \(4\) and fixes every other vertex maps \(Q_1\)'s edges onto \(Q_2\)'s one by one -- \(1\!-\!2\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!1\!-\!2\), \(2\!-\!3\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!2\!-\!4\), \(3\!-\!4\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!4\!-\!3\), \(4\!-\!5\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!3\!-\!5\), \(4\!-\!6\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!3\!-\!6\), \(6\!-\!7\!\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow \!6\!-\!7\) -- exactly the listed set of \(Q_2\). The tempting rule ``root both at the smaller-labelled centre endpoint \(3\)'' fails: \(\Phi _3(Q_1)=\langle \)chain-of-two, \(\langle \text  {leaf},\text  {chain-of-two}\rangle \rangle \) has \emph  {two} children while \(\Phi _3(Q_2)=\langle \)leaf, chain-of-two, chain-of-three\(\rangle \) has \emph  {three} -- different names, so a single-endpoint rule answers NO on a YES instance. The sorted pair of \(Definition~\ref {def:c22-canroot}\) is the fix: \(\{\Phi _3,\Phi _4\}\) is the same multiset for both trees, only with the roles of \(3\) and \(4\) exchanged.}}{1674}{figure.caption.284}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c22-pair}{{28.3.1}{1674}{The centre-edge trap, with a real counterexample. \(Q_1\) has edges \(1\!-\!2,2\!-\!3,3\!-\!4,4\!-\!5,4\!-\!6,6\!-\!7\); \(Q_2\) has \(1\!-\!2,2\!-\!4,4\!-\!3,3\!-\!5,3\!-\!6,6\!-\!7\). Both centres are the edge \(\{3,4\}\) (red), and \(Q_1\cong Q_2\): the bijection that swaps \(3\) and \(4\) and fixes every other vertex maps \(Q_1\)'s edges onto \(Q_2\)'s one by one -- \(1\!-\!2\!\mapsto \!1\!-\!2\), \(2\!-\!3\!\mapsto \!2\!-\!4\), \(3\!-\!4\!\mapsto \!4\!-\!3\), \(4\!-\!5\!\mapsto \!3\!-\!5\), \(4\!-\!6\!\mapsto \!3\!-\!6\), \(6\!-\!7\!\mapsto \!6\!-\!7\) -- exactly the listed set of \(Q_2\). The tempting rule ``root both at the smaller-labelled centre endpoint \(3\)'' fails: \(\Phi _3(Q_1)=\langle \)chain-of-two, \(\langle \text {leaf},\text {chain-of-two}\rangle \rangle \) has \emph {two} children while \(\Phi _3(Q_2)=\langle \)leaf, chain-of-two, chain-of-three\(\rangle \) has \emph {three} -- different names, so a single-endpoint rule answers NO on a YES instance. The sorted pair of \(\defref {c22-canroot}\) is the fix: \(\{\Phi _3,\Phi _4\}\) is the same multiset for both trees, only with the roles of \(3\) and \(4\) exchanged}{figure.caption.284}{}}
